  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nWidespread light rain has developed across west central MN with\na few showers extending to the southeast into the Mankato and\nAlbert Lea areas. Coverage of this rain is far exceeding model\noutput, but a dry boundary layer should limit most rainfall \ntotals to a tenth of an inch or less. Coverage will decrease\nthis evening but occasional light rain is expected across the\nregion, particularly across western MN. \n\nAn upper low over the Four Corners region this afternoon will \nadvance to SD Monday afternoon. As it approaches, rain coverage \nwill increase across the central Plains to southern and western \nMN by tonight. The surface low will track from eastern NE to \ncentral MN Monday into Tuesday. This is a shift to the east from\nyesterday. The best moisture transport will be confined to \nMN and is where the best chances for a soaking rain is forecast.\nRainfall totals of around an inch are likely across western and\ncentral MN with amounts tapering off to the southeast. The \nsystem will exit to the north early Tuesday. Another disturbance\nwill quickly follow Tuesday night through Wednesday night, \nbringing the next chance for showers. Amounts with this round\nwill be lighter - a quarter inch or less.\n\nA ridge will remain parked over the southeastern U.S. through \nthe rest of the forecast period. Surface high pressure building\ninto the northern Plains will lead to a drier forecast Thursday,\nbut moisture will increase on the backside of the high Friday\nand into the weekend. Predictability beyond midweek remains low\nwith considerable spread in the guidance. \n\nTemperatures will be cool Monday, but then will warm back to \nthe 60s and quite possibly 70s from Tuesday onward. Given the \ncurrent forecast numbers, it appears MSP will finish this month \nas the 2nd warmest October on record.",
